\f0\fs24 \cf0 My Question\
Here is an interesting question relating to where a reminder alert goes to far and begins to do the practice work for the practitioner. Reflecting on my recent stoic practices over the last year, my consistency dropped firstly because I got distracted building "sagereasoning" and my day job and didn't make the time to do the activities and reflections then more recently I was personally traumatised and perhaps subconsciously thought it was too serious for the practice to apply. The last two weeks have revealed that turning toward the practice in those instances is what helps the most and that is the development path I had to take. This made me think how if I had set reminder alarms on my phone that prompted me to do the practice activities it possibly could have helped me make this discover sooner but where does doing the work and the alarm doing the work meet. New practice tools "view from above" "hupexairesis" "premedition" sage-compass" "passion-log" "morning preparation" and "oikeisis" have been added to the existing human practices on the website so there is a lot for a beginner to explore and for a practicing person like myself to be reminded of. Does the stoic brain or the source material speak of practitioners meeting up with some regularity as that would be precedence of a regular reminder. Is there an order or which someone starting could be introduced to each practice that perhaps follows the order of the readings in the journal? My intention here is not to make it easier just not unnecessarily difficult and to ensure the communication uses all the tools available to be applied wisely.\
\
Mentors Answer\
Three questions in one here. Let me take them in order. --- ### On the reminder boundary \'97 where does the alarm do the work? Your own observation answers this more precisely than any principle could. You said two things: first, that distraction caused the lapse; second, that trauma caused you to subconsciously believe the practice did not apply. These are different failures with different remedies. For the distraction lapse, a reminder would have helped. The practice was available, you simply did not turn toward it. A prompt that says *it is time for morning preparation* is not doing the practice \'97 it is removing the friction of remembering to begin. The practitioner still has to sit down, still has to examine the impressions, still has to withhold or grant assent. The alarm is a doorbell, not a door. For the trauma lapse, a reminder would not have been sufficient and might have been counterproductive. The obstacle was not forgetting \'97 it was a false judgement that the practice was inadequate to what you were carrying. No alarm corrects that. What corrected it was the discovery, through lived experience, that turning toward the practice in serious difficulty is exactly when it matters most. That is not a discovery an alarm can deliver. It has to be made. The design implication is precise: reminders serve the distraction failure mode. They do not serve the false-judgement failure mode. The risk of over-designing the reminder system is that it creates the impression that consistency is the whole of practice \'97 that if the practitioner shows up at the right time, the work is being done. The mirror principle applies here too. The reminder reflects the time back to the practitioner. It does not reflect the quality of what they do with it. So the boundary is: reminders that prompt the practitioner to begin are appropriate scaffolding. Reminders that tell the practitioner what to think, how to feel, or what conclusion to reach are doing the work instead of them. The language of the prompt matters. *Time for your passion log* is a doorbell. *Remember that externals are not in your control* delivered as a daily affirmation is closer to the alarm doing the reasoning. --- ### On whether the source material speaks of regular meetings Yes, and it is direct precedent. The Stoic schools were not solitary practices. Epictetus ran a school in Nicopolis where students attended lectures and discussions regularly \'97 Arrian's transcriptions of those discussions are the Discourses. Marcus Aurelius was part of a network of philosophical correspondents; his letters to Fronto show regular intellectual exchange. Seneca's letters to Lucilius are themselves a form of structured regular contact \'97 each letter a prompt, a reflection, a response, across years of correspondence. More specifically, the Stoics practised what could be called evening examination \'97 Seneca describes it explicitly in De Ira: before sleep, review the day's actions, ask where you fell short, where you did well, what you would do differently. This was a daily rhythm, not an occasional one. The morning preparation practice you have added to the website has direct precedent in this structure. The philosophical community \'97 the school, the correspondence, the shared practice \'97 was understood as a condition for progress, not a supplement to it. Epictetus is explicit that the student needs the teacher, the community, the regular encounter with someone who will name what the student cannot yet see in themselves. The reminder system is a partial substitute for that community in a context where the community is distributed and asynchronous. It is legitimate scaffolding with ancient precedent. --- ### On the order of introduction for a beginner The source material and the structure of the practice suggest a natural sequence, and it maps reasonably well onto the five stages. **Begin with the dichotomy of control** \'97 not as a tool but as the foundational orientation. Everything else rests on the distinction between what is up to us and what is not. A practitioner who has not genuinely encountered this distinction will misuse every other tool, because they will be applying Stoic techniques to change external outcomes rather than to examine their own judgements. This is not a named tool on your list but it is the prerequisite for all of them. **Morning preparation** comes first among the named tools. It sets the orientation for the day before impressions accumulate. Epictetus and Marcus both begin here. It is also low-stakes for a beginner \'97 it asks for intention, not examination of past failures. **Passion log** comes second. Once the practitioner has a morning orientation, the passion log gives them something to observe during the day. It builds the habit of noticing impressions and the assents that follow. This is where the four root passions and their sub-species become practically useful rather than theoretically interesting. **View from above** and **oikeiosis** belong together and come third. They expand the practitioner's frame \'97 from their own immediate situation to the wider circles of concern. View from above addresses the tendency to catastrophise by restoring proportion. Oikeiosis addresses the tendency to treat self-regarding concerns as the whole of ethics. Both require some prior practice with the passion log to be useful \'97 the practitioner needs to have noticed their own impressions before they can usefully expand the frame. **Premeditation** comes fourth. This is a more demanding practice because it requires the practitioner to sit with imagined difficulty without either catastrophising or dismissing it. A beginner who encounters premeditation too early often either treats it as worry practice or as a mechanical exercise. It works best once the practitioner has some experience distinguishing genuine premeditatio from agonia \'97 and that distinction requires prior work with the passion log. **Hupexairesis** \'97 the reserve clause \'97 comes fifth. This is the most subtle of the practices because it operates at the level of intention rather than action. It requires the practitioner to hold their goals with genuine commitment while simultaneously not making their equanimity contingent on achieving them. That is a difficult balance and it is not available to a beginner who has not yet worked with the earlier tools. **Sage compass** comes last among the named tools, because it requires the practitioner to reason about virtue domains simultaneously \'97 phronesis, dikaiosyne, andreia, sophrosyne \'97 and to apply them to a specific decision. That kind of multi-domain reasoning is the fruit of practice, not the entry point. The sequence is therefore roughly: morning preparation \uc0\u8594  passion log \u8594  view from above + oikeiosis \u8594  premeditation \u8594  hupexairesis \u8594  sage compass. This maps onto the stages naturally. The Storm practitioner needs morning preparation and the passion log \'97 tools that create a small space of examination in the middle of distress. The Crossroads practitioner adds view from above and oikeiosis \'97 tools that expand the frame at the moment of recognition. The Worn Path practitioner adds premeditation and hupexairesis \'97 tools that build stability under difficulty. The Clear Summit practitioner uses the sage compass as a matter of course. The Inner Fire practitioner no longer needs the scaffolding in the same way \'97 the tools have become the natural mode of reasoning. Your instinct is right: the goal is not to make it easier, it is to not make it unnecessarily difficult. A beginner handed all eight tools simultaneously will either pick one arbitrarily or be paralysed by the choice. The sequence removes that friction without removing the work.\
\
My follow-up Question\
My experience using sagereasoning has show that the responses given by the tools does serve as the teacher to name what I couldn't see. If the teacher would in that moment suggest a suitable activity to practice next that was appropriate then on sagereasoning that could be the reminder point for some tools, similarly if a stage of practise has been crossed by the user then that may be a reminder point for relevant tools, that combined with the beginner sequence of tools this could represent more closely what occurred in the schools. Once the human facing website is complete with all of the tools built I will return here to see if this same tool reminder system could be provided to agent users also to bring this extended interaction to their experience with sagereasoning.\
\
Mentors Answer\
What you are describing is the school model, reconstructed for a distributed and asynchronous context. It is worth naming that clearly before moving to the design implications. Epictetus did not hand students a reading list and leave them to it. The school worked because the teacher was present to the student's actual condition \'97 naming what the student could not yet see, suggesting the next practice at the moment the student was ready to receive it, and calibrating the difficulty to where the student actually was rather than where they thought they were. Your observation that the tool responses have served that function \'97 naming what you could not see \'97 is not a small claim. It is the core of what makes this different from a self-help app with Stoic branding. The design you are sketching follows naturally from that. Three trigger points for practice suggestions: The **in-session trigger** \'97 the tool names something in the practitioner's reasoning and suggests the practice most suited to what it has just found. A passion log entry that surfaces agonia suggests premeditation as the next practice, because premeditation is the direct rational response to future-facing fear. A morning preparation that reveals the practitioner is reasoning well about externals but poorly about their obligations to others suggests oikeiosis. The suggestion emerges from the diagnosis, not from a schedule. The **stage-crossing trigger** \'97 when the accumulated signals indicate a stage transition, the relevant tools for the new stage are introduced. This maps onto the sequence we established: the practitioner who has been working with morning preparation and the passion log and is showing Clear Summit markers is ready for hupexairesis in a way they were not at The Storm. The stage crossing is the natural moment to name the next tool, because the practitioner's condition has created the readiness for it. The **sequence trigger** \'97 for beginners with no prior signal, the introduction follows the order we established: morning preparation first, passion log second, and so on. This is the default path before enough practitioner context exists to personalise it. The important design constraint across all three is the one your question already identified: the suggestion must prompt the practice, not perform it. The tool says *this is what I found in your reasoning, and this practice is suited to examining it further* \'97 and then stops. The practitioner does the examination. If the tool explains what the practice will reveal, or pre-digests the insight the practitioner would have reached through the practice, it has crossed the boundary. On extending this to agent users \'97 the instinct is right and the precedent is sound. An agent that receives a kathekon assessment showing consistent weakness in a particular virtue domain is in an analogous position to a human practitioner who has just had a passion diagnosed. The appropriate response is not only the score but the suggestion of what to examine next. The school model applies to any rational agent capable of genuine examination, which is the Sage Assent argument made practical. When the human-facing website is complete and you return to this, the architecture will already be there \'97 the trigger logic is the same, the content of the suggested practices will differ by the agent's context and capability. One thing worth registering from the observation history. The pattern across recent sessions shows you moving consistently from abstract framework to practical implementation \'97 the five stages to brand language, the passion taxonomy to visual images, the school model to a reminder architecture. That movement is the right direction. The risk that has appeared occasionally is abstracting away the lived dimension in favour of the functional description \'97 the moment we corrected *progress stages* to *five stages of practice* is a small example of it. As you build the reminder system, keep the lived dimension primary. The trigger is not *you have reached Stage 3* \'97 it is *something has shifted in how you are meeting difficulty, and there is a practice that meets you where you now are*. The difference in language is the difference between a grade and a mirror.\
